WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:05.440
Set against the tumultuous&nbsp;
backdrop of the 1967 Newark Riots,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:12.240
"The Many Saints of Newark" is a prequel to "The&nbsp;
Sopranos," HBO's smash hit prestige TV series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:18.560
that became a cultural phenomenon when it debuted&nbsp;
back in 1999. The film is co-written by David&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:23.760
Chase, who created the original "Sopranos"&nbsp;
series and its colorful cast of characters.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:29.680
Starring James Gandolfini's son Michael Gandolfini&nbsp;
as young Tony Soprano, "The Many Saints of Newark"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:34.480
chronicles the formative years of Tony as&nbsp;
a kid and a teenager, and the impactful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:40.320
relationship he shared with his mob boss uncle&nbsp;
Dickie Moltisanti. As fans well know, it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:46.560
not exactly be a spoiler to say that young Tony&nbsp;
grows up to follow in Uncle Dickie's footsteps.

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:52.320
So how does this improbable prequel end?&nbsp;
Does the ending serve as a logical prologue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:58.160
to "The Sopranos"? Or is it telling its own&nbsp;
story, simply set in David Chase's universe?

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:01.840
The opening shot gently pans&nbsp;
over a gloomy graveyard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:06.640
getting closer and closer to what is&nbsp;
revealed to be Christopher's grave -- yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:10.480
that Christopher. In the first few&nbsp;
shots of the movie, the audience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:15.200
hears the voice of Christopher Moltisanti,&nbsp;
telling the story from beyond the grave.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:20.720
Since Christopher is a baby in the time period&nbsp;
that "Many Saints" takes place, he hardly factors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:26.240
into the prequel story. Tony exclaims that his&nbsp;
baby nephew only ever cries when he is around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:29.760
pretty deftly foreshadowing their&nbsp;
tumultuous future relationship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:33.520
which takes a murderous turn during&nbsp;
the timeline of "The Sopranos."

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:39.840
"Some babies, when they come into the world,&nbsp;
know all kinds of things from the other side."&nbsp;

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:46.320
These little referential treats aside, the&nbsp;
story of "Many Saints" doesn't really turn out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:52.320
to be about Tony or Christopher by the end. It's&nbsp;
got a lot more to do with the man whose shadow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:58.240
extends over their relationship for decades to&nbsp;
come: Christopher's father, Dickie Moltisanti.

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:01.120
While the movie is mostly framed&nbsp;
through young Tony's point of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:05.120
view as he experiences the chaos and&nbsp;
violence surrounding him and his family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:09.920
the story is really more focused on the trials&nbsp;
and tribulations of Dickie Moltisanti's life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:13.440
In an interview with The Hollywood&nbsp;
Reporter, director Alan Taylor said,

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:16.720
"It was important to feel [Tony] powerfully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:20.640
in the movie even though&nbsp;
structurally it's Dickie's story."

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:25.840
This certainly seems to suggest that the focus&nbsp;
on Dickie is just as the creators intended.&nbsp;

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:31.120
This is part of the reason Christopher narrates&nbsp;
instead of our present-day POV character, young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:36.720
Tony. Tony's environment and his pivotal bond with&nbsp;
Dickie essentially morphs him into the New Jersey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:42.800
mob boss that he infamously becomes, but "Many&nbsp;
Saints" isn't really a Tony Soprano origin story.

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:46.880
At the crux of the film are the tensions&nbsp;
between the white and Black residents of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:51.440
Newark, New Jersey after a Black man is&nbsp;
killed by the Newark police, a real life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:55.840
incident that resulted in uprisings&nbsp;
that nearly burned the city down.

00:02:55.840 --> 00:03:00.960
The generational conflict between the mob and&nbsp;
the Black community is a constant presence in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:05.760
"Many Saints," with the heightened racial&nbsp;
tensions serving as more setting than plot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:12.160
point. This ambient tension is made manifest in&nbsp;
the story via the looming conflict between Dickie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:16.240
and a Black man named Harold McBrayer&nbsp;
with whom he used to do business.

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:22.560
When McBrayer turns against Dickie for killing one&nbsp;
of his own, an all out war ensues between the two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:28.720
rival factions of Newark. It becomes a blood bath&nbsp;
between the Italian mob and McBrayer and his crew.

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:34.240
If racial and cultural tensions were always&nbsp;
simmering beneath the surface on "The Sopranos,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:37.680
they claim top billing in&nbsp;
the plot of "Many Saints."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:43.280
Even though the film is a period piece, this&nbsp;
choice makes it feel current and culturally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:48.400
relevant, and may even provide a fresh lens&nbsp;
through which to revisit "The Sopranos."

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:53.440
Interestingly, the film's culminating act&nbsp;
of violence isn't an act of racial animus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:57.840
but rather a manifestation of the&nbsp;
conflicts within the Italian mob.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:03.120
This dynamic, too, will be familiar to fans&nbsp;
of "The Sopranos" -- no matter how many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:08.800
threats attack from the outside, it's always the&nbsp;
threats from within that prove the most deadly.

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:14.240
Toward the end of the film, in a surprise twist,&nbsp;
Dickie is suddenly shot and killed by someone who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:19.120
is revealed to have been hired by Junior Soprano,&nbsp;
Tony's uncle and a member of the family who has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:25.120
always been at odds with Dickie. Earlier, Dickie&nbsp;
even humiliates Junior in a very public way by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:28.880
laughing at him when he falls down the&nbsp;
stairs at Dickie's father's funeral.

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:33.440
It's interesting to consider the ways&nbsp;
this struggle between Junior and Dickie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:38.960
replays a generation later between Junior and&nbsp;
Tony — Junior tries to kill Tony in Season 1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:43.680
you'll recall — and how this twist&nbsp;
reveals the truth about Barry Haydu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:48.240
the retired cop who Christoper thinks killed&nbsp;
his father, because Tony convinced him of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:51.920
this … and who Christopher murders&nbsp;
in his home at the start of Season 4.

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:57.520
"You're being set up! He's&nbsp;
lying to you, whoever he is!"

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:03.120
At Dickie's funeral, Tony stands staring&nbsp;
at Dickie in the open casket. He imagines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:07.600
himself making a pinky swear with Dickie. It's&nbsp;
a callback to a moment earlier in the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:12.880
but more importantly, it foreshadows the&nbsp;
way Tony will continue Dickie's legacy.

